---
---
<section id="how-i-work" class="scroll-mt-20 px-6 py-24 md:py-32" aria-label="How I Work">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">
    <div class="mb-12 flex items-center gap-4">
      <div
        class="flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl text-primary"
        style="box-shadow: var(--neu-raised-sm)"
      >
        <span class="text-sm font-bold">03</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-muted-foreground">
        How I Work
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div
      class="rounded-2xl bg-background p-8 md:p-12"
      style="box-shadow: var(--neu-raised)"
    >
      <h3 class="mb-6 text-balance text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-4xl">
        AI &amp; <span class="text-primary">adaptability</span>.
      </h3>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        <p>
          The way I work has changed more in the last two years than in the
          previous ten. AI hasn't just added new tools to my workflow — it has
          fundamentally reshaped how I think about building products. From ideation
          to code, from user research to deployment, I've integrated AI as a
          thinking partner rather than a shortcut.
        </p>
        <p>
          But adopting AI is only half the story. The real shift has been in
          mindset: learning to stay flexible, questioning established processes,
          and being willing to throw away what worked yesterday if something works
          better today. I've had to unlearn as much as I've learned — and that's
          been the most valuable part.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="my-8 rounded-xl bg-background p-6"
        style="box-shadow: var(--neu-inset)"
      >
        <blockquote class="text-center text-lg font-medium italic text-foreground">
          "The best tool is the one that makes you rethink how you work."
        </blockquote>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        <p>
          I don't just practice this myself — I promote it across my team and
          company. Leading by example means showing, not telling. It means pairing
          with engineers to explore AI-assisted development, running experiments
          with product workflows, and creating space for the team to adapt at
          their own pace. The goal isn't to chase trends; it's to build a culture
          where continuous learning is the default.
        </p>
        <p>
          Technology moves fast. The engineers and product leaders who thrive
          aren't the ones who know the most — they're the ones who adapt the
          fastest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
